<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcianopolis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marcianopolis was a Roman city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>, near modern Devnya in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> and named after his sister Marciana. It became the capital of the province of Moesia Secunda. In the 3rd century it was besieged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and Carpi, and in 376–377 it was the scene of the clash that began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-376-382">Gothic War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcianopolis.jpg" alt="Marcianopolis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rjdeadly · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marcianopolis_city_model.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was given its name after the Second Dacian War, which ended in 106, in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s sister Ulpia Marciana. An important strategic centre, it belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracia">Roman Thrace</a> until 187 to 193 and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia-inferior">Moesia Inferior</a>. Its prosperity under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severan-dynasty">Severan dynasty</a> was ended by a Gothic raid in 248 to 249 and by later invasions from the north, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> repulsed another Gothic attack on the town in 267 or 268, in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a>.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> it was rebuilt thoroughly in the late 3rd and early 4th centuries and grew at the expense of neighbouring Odessos, the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a>. It was an important episcopal centre, and during the war of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> from 366 to 369 it served as a temporary capital of the empire; a source of the time calls it the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> destroyed it in 447, immediately after the battle of the Utus river. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> restored and fortified it.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> sacked it in 587, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> retook it at once, and the Byzantine army was quartered there in 596 before crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> against them. An Avar raid finally destroyed the city in 614 to 615, though it was still marked on maps long after.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> who settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> in the 7th century called the ruins Devina, and the site lies at modern Devnya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Partly excavated, it has an amphitheatre, streets and a basilica found in the 20th century. The mosaic floors of the House of Antiope, a villa of the late 3rd or early 4th century, are shown in the Museum of Mosaics, some of them where they were laid.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marcianopolis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the crisis of the 3rd century they joined the Goths in raids across the Danube, besieging <b>Marcianopolis</b> and attacking Dacia.</blockquote><a href="/state/carpi">Carpi</a></li></ul></section>
